No, ROP (Return-Oriented Programming) is actually the first stage of Binary Exploitation (Pwn), not Web Hacking.
When you move into ROP, you have completely stepped out of the web browser layers and entered the world of low-level operating system memory, CPU architecture, and assembly code.
To understand where you are in your security journey, it helps to see how the industry splits these hacking specializations into stages and layers:
## 🌐 The 3 Layers of Hacking

[ Layer 1: Web Application Hacking ]  <-- (Where you started)
       │   - Tricking database syntax (SQLi)
       │   - Swapping HTTP headers (Cache Poisoning)
       │   - Manipulating file paths (Path Traversal)
       │
[ Layer 2: Infrastructure & Cloud ]  <-- (The bridge)
       │   - Pivoting networks (SSRF)
       │   - Attacking dependencies (Supply Chain Attacks)
       │
[ Layer 3: Binary & Memory Exploitation ] <-- (Where ROP lives)
           - Overwriting CPU hardware registers
           - Modifying RAM stacks and memory addresses

------------------------------
## 🚨 The Ultimate Intersection: How Web Leads to ROP
Even though ROP is a binary exploit, a web hacker will use it when they hit the absolute limit of a web application.
Here is exactly how a real-world critical attack chain bridges the two worlds together:

   1. The Web Entry (Stage 1): You find a Path Traversal or File Upload vulnerability in a web application dashboard.
   2. The Delivery (Stage 2): You use that vulnerability to upload a file to the server. But instead of uploading a standard web script, you upload a malicious binary payload into an application directory.
   3. The Memory Breach (Stage 3): The backend server processes your file using a custom data utility compiled in C or C++. Your file triggers a buffer overflow in that local utility, bypassing the CPU's memory protections.
   4. The ROP Execution (Stage 4): You use an ROP Chain to hijack the local utility’s execution, forcing it to talk back to your machine and give you a root terminal shell.

Once you reach the ROP stage, you have successfully transformed from a web hunter into a binary exploit developer.
